From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Sun, 4 Dec 1994 18:34:24 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-134] Machineries, etc.

Fred Hapgood asks..

>...Let's say I want to live in a society that prohibits child abuse
>and am willing to pay for my preferences.  How do I do that under
>Friedman's scheme?  

Tim Starr says...

   I'd imagine that the first thing you'd do is move into your
   own proprietary community in which "child abuse" defined
   the way you like would be prohibited.

Fred Hapgood, deeply confused, replies:

>What??  Move?  Why would I want to move?  Why wouldn't I just pay
>guys to go around and take kids away from parents who are giving
>them the wrong kind of attention?  

Starr repostes:

      It's cheaper.

Huh?  Suppose I live at address A.  I contribute $25 a month to
the 'Save-the-children' fund.  This fund finances a firm of PIs
who specialize in snatching children who are being mistreated by
their parents and placing them in homes with the right
atmosphere.  Now I move to address B, a neighborhood where there
are even more people who believe even more strongly in fighting
child abuse.  What is there about that move that is going to
incline me to contribute less?  If anything, I would contribute
more!

There is a tone here that seems to suggest that given the choice,
people would choose laws they want to live under -- to apply to
themselves.  Surely that is unintended.  Nobody wants laws to
apply to themselves. It seems to me that if and when private law
enforcement appears the big products are going come from
promising to impose laws on others.  Friedman makes the point
that this would be expensive, but we are talking here about
cultural statements, not specific products, and these can be
bought incrementally.  Curing AIDS is expensive, but that doesn't
prevent people from donating money to the cause.  Besides, lots
of these causes could draw on volunteer energy.  There are people
in my neighborhood would be happy to hire a dog doo cop if and
only if he would promise to drag offenders in front of them for a
public horsewhipping.  If they couldn't afford his services they
would be happy to volunteer.

Nick Szabo says:
>
>I presume that enforcement of a contract is not considered
>initiation of force...

Starr pounces:

      Bingo!

When you have a dispute, one side always sees the act of imposing
compliance as a simple matter of 'enforcing the contract' (and
therefore not coercion), while the other side sees it just as
certainly as imposing a false reading of the contract, or a
contract that was never agreed to, or was defective in some
fashion, and therefore coercion.  While it might be true in an
abstract and technical sense that enforcement of a contract is
not coercion, in real life, when at least some of the disputes
you get into are going to be decided against you, over your
furious objections, the distinction soon evaporates.

