From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Sun, 04 Dec 1994 17:57:34 -0600
Subject: [#94-12-135] MEDIA: Radio show impressions

At 02:03 PM 12/4/374, vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner) wrote:
[FQA Radio interview]
> I am a little concerned about what kind of first
>impression Extropianism makes on the average person.  This morning's
>interview is a good example.  In order, the topics raised seemed to be:
>
>  1. Leather.
>  2. Caller-ID.
>  3. Head-freezing.

This is what tends to happen in a short radio interview (I haven't heard this
one in particular -- I'd be willing to pay a modest fee for a tape or
transcript, hint). The host, or a caller if it's a call-in show, will seize
on the first controversial point that comes up, and everyone is left hoping
for spontaneous order, because it often seems everyone has lost control.

One way out of this is to have a short list of points that you want to
cover, and use the politician's answer method (when asked a question,
give your prepared remarks as an answer, and leave the questioner to
figure out if you addressed the question or not) to make sure they get
covered. 

[VK prefers PHIL/TECH/ECON to POL, and letting the right folks find Extropians]

I agree with Vince but I'd like to point out why discussions of TECH/ECON
slip into POL: exactly because one wants to keep the discussions grounded in
the practical, and so many of the limiting factors today are political in
nature. As soon as we bring up free banking or free-market economics, we
are drawn into a discussion of how to get there from here. I believe that
this comes with the territory (or with the first-level map, _pace_ g.s. :-)
and that it's not all bad.

Now, about marketing -- we (ExI) certainly are benefitted by marketing
_Extropy_, and selling out a big press run of the _Big Yellow Book_, or
whatever the working title is now, would be a coup: we could all bask in
the reflected glow from D/R/G's money bins; but we probably don't need
John Q. Public on the email list or as ExI members.

OTOH, there's no algorithm for selecting exactly the team of innovators
who will make the breakthroughs that we want, so what can we do but put
our best manipulators forward and let SO work?

