Date: Sun, 4 Dec 1994 19:28:43 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-136] Machineries...

John Clark says:

      >>People won't be willing to pay to restrain the private
      >>behavior of others in most areas, child abuse and perhaps a
      >>few others excepted.

Fred Hapgood sez back:

     >Like I say, we must live on different planets. [...] --
     >private groups can raise millions in donations on the promise
     >that they will do precisely that.

John Clark replies:

      The prude is considering 2 Private Protection Agencies
      (PPA) that are identical except for one thing, one has a
      decency patrol to harass women on the beach 20 miles from
      your home, the other doesn't but uses the money saved to
      send an extra security patrol through your neighborhood at
      3Am. The prude would have to be very prudish indeed to pick
      the first PPA.

You must live in a nice part of the country.  Where I live the
second PPA would do a fine business.  But that's not the right
analogy anyway.  People probably would hire one PPA to do the
basic neighborhood security scutwork, like the police do now, and
then they would make donations to some other group promising to
"protect family values".  It would be this other group that would
go harass the nude bathers or beat up gays (or spike trees or
fling paint on fur coats or yell at smokers or whatever).

FH:
     >Suppose they *hadn't* so contracted?  Suppose they had
     >missed the last payment?

JC:
      Suppose they don't spend money for food? Better look for
      some charity.
	  
In other words, they _would_ be getting the bullets and beatings.
Fine.  I'm not objecting to any of this.  I just wanted to point
out that whatever virtues this anarcho-capitalist utopia thing
might have, freedom from coercion is not one of them.  It might
in fact be a fairly brutal, tense, and unpleasant system.  We
have no way of knowing.  On the basis of the answers I've been
getting here, though, I sure wouldn't want to rush into the 
experiment.  Personally.  

Robin Hanson writes reflexively:

      Well most people I know have radically different
      preferencs, but if this is the kind of law folks in your
      area would prefer, why shouldn't it be the kind of law they
      have?  Who are you to tell them they should have some other
      kind of law?

I never did.

