From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Sun, 4 Dec 1994 17:06:05 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-138] MEDIA: Radio show impressions

szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo) sez:

>Naturally, leather & head-freezing happen to be the flashiest, hippest,
>and most outrageous things that the media will want to cover.

That may be true, but shouldn't we steer the conversation in the direction
we want it to go, rather than follow the whim of the media?  I know this is
hard to do, so it may be a matter of practice and experience.

>Caller
>ID seemd to be an issue which was rudely carried over from the previous
>show

I wondered where that came from, since it didn't strike me as a very
Extropian topic (although it is a privacy issue).

>Alas, these can't be covered in 5-30 second sound bites

Isn't that all the more reason to be very specific and concise in the time
available?  I always tell people that Extropians are those who are serious
about doing away with death and taxes, and go from there (assuming I still
have an audience).  While I think "Team FQA" did a good job today, I am
concerned about the kind of impression "Joe Public" gets from any 15-minute
cold intro which includes interruptions for commercials, etc...  We'll have
to see what kind of response we get from the program, assuming we can
quantify that.

>As far as I can tell, anarcho-capitalist politics has always been
>part of the agenda.

Oh really?  I had thought that the primary relationship between
Extropianism and politics was that we were against all forms of government
and taxation as being restrictions on "Boundless Expansion", e.g.  It seems
strange to me that we would advocate any particular type of political
system in the long run.  I realize that this topic is a hot one on the
list, so this may just be a matter of semantics, since anarchy means
"without rules or restrictions".

>The sensational issues are probably necessary
>for media exposure.

I guess this is the problem I have: nobody has successfully explained to me
why we need media exposure.  We're not Scientology, after all ;-).

>I plead guilty to over-emphasis of medium-term, gee-whiz technologies
>such as space colonization, advanced manufacturing, et. al.

Huh?  I thought the topics like space colonization and advanced
manufacturing *were* the important issues!

>On the other
>hand, the theoretical applied science, and its consequences on quality
>and span of life, are crucial to the Extropian worldview.

Yes.  The primary key Extropian issue as I see it is: "Taking Immortality
And  Its Consequences Seriously".  Once one does this, all the other issues
fall into place.  The whole point of government-bashing, head-freezing, and
nanotech seems to me to be aimed at eliminating this entropic conspiracy
called "death".

>perhaps our biggest gap is the need for a practical
>Extropianism that stresses personal success in the today's environment

Agreed.  I've always thought of Extropianism as a personal, rather than a
collectivist, philosophy, since it stresses individualist values and
opportunities.  Given the long way we have to go before we can live
forever, practical ways to cope with current and intermediate stages of
existence (and of potential non-existence) are a necessity.

>The most sucessful life, I posit, will be
>lived where one's worry space roughly corresponds to one's influence
>space.

When we reach the point where our influence space equals our worry space,
the latter will disappear.  This does seem to be our ultimate goal:
complete and utter control over everything.  The elimination of death is
the primary hurdle along the way.

--Vince

