Subject: [#94-12-139] BASICS Re: Social Security

Betsy says that she believes Friedman is wrong when he says the
Social Security benefits the wealthy more than the poor. Well, 
facts tends to side here with Friedman. Let me explain:
- Only a certain portion of earnings, "from the bottom" has always
been subject to FICA tax. My wife works with the payroll of a 
department of the School of Medicine at a university, and is amused
how new professors always announce their salary to the stuff
by coming to the office and wondering why Social Security tax was no
longer deducted from their paycheck. The time when they do so
reveals their salary.
- The benefit formula is tilted in favor of the poor, but the wealthy
live longer, so they collect more nevertheless.
- The claim that "without Social Security people would be starving"
sounds appealing, but cannot possibly be proven. On the other hand,
how much unemployment is caused by the payroll tax? Remember, that tax
falls on the employer and employee, and sharply increases cost of
labor, so only the lucky ones whose productivity exceeds the 
government mandated minimum can be employed. "lucky" they not
necessarily are, virtous rather, but government insists that
those below the line need not be employed, and then you'll see them
on the streets (hopefully with squegees -- did I spell it right?).
So -- without Social Security people could be starving,
hypothetically, but with Social Security people definitely are
starving.
- Why is it that it is so easy to believe that government's 
robbery first, welfare checks second, is the only way for poor people
to be taken care of? Well, it used to be that children took
care of their parents in their old days. Now they don't have to,
the government will. And the elderly scared stiff must vote 
to keep their program checks coming.

That's called "humanitarian".

